 {
  "key": "series.arithmetic.q17",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amath.series.arithmetic"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "Prove that the sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $a$ and common difference $d$ is $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 writes $S_n$ forwards and backwards (in terms of $a$, $d$ and the last term $l = a + (n - 1)d$).",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 adds term by term: each of the $n$ pairs sums to $2a + (n - 1)d$, so $2S_n = n\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 divides by 2 to reach the result.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\nS_n = a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) + \\cdots + [a + (n - 1)d]\n$$\n\n$$\nS_n = [a + (n - 1)d] + [a + (n - 2)d] + \\cdots + a\n$$\n\nAdding, each of the $n$ columns gives $2a + (n - 1)d$:\n\n$$\n2S_n = n\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}\n$$\n\n$$\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2}\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Reversing the series pairs the first term with the last, the second with the second-last, and so on; every pair has the same total.",
  "hints": [
   "Write the sum in reverse order underneath and add the two lines."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
